---
/**
 * The corrections policy and the dated log it commits to. Newest first. An entry is
 * here only if the error changed what the site recommends or what a reader would do
 * about it; decimals and wording are fixed beside the number they belong to.
 *
 * Every heading id is a permanent anchor. Every date is the day the corrected figure
 * went live, which is checkable in the commit history.
 */
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections"
  description="What this site got wrong, when it was fixed, and what it says now. Every date is the day the corrected figure went live."
>
  <Hero
    title="Corrections"
    lede="What this site got wrong, when it was fixed, and what it says now."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>When a number on this site turns out to be wrong, it is corrected everywhere it appears and an entry
    lands here with the date. A withdrawn number is deleted rather than replaced with a better guess, because a
    blank looks like an oversight and a plausible number looks like work.</p>

    <p>Most errors are decimals and wording, and those are fixed in silence beside the number they belong to.
    The entries below are the ones that changed what the site recommends or what a reader would do about it.
    Newest first.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="log">The log</h2>

    <h3 id="behaviour-gap-attribution">A number credited to the wrong source</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-24</p>
    <p>The gap between what a fund earns and what its investors earn was attributed to Morningstar. The figure
    was mine, from a simulation; Morningstar&rsquo;s own is much larger and describes a population. Each is now
    quoted at its own size under its own name.</p>

    <h3 id="timing-benchmark">A timing rule measured against one thing and reported against another</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-24</p>
    <p>The 200-day rule was said to beat holding by <Figure id="timing-rule-gap" inline showUnit={false} />
    points a year. That gap is against a blend holding the same average amount in stocks; against simply
    holding, the rule was 0.73 points a year behind. Both readings now appear together.</p>

    <h3 id="rebalancing-policy">A rebalancing cost that belonged to a different portfolio</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-24</p>
    <p>A cost of 0.387% a year was quoted for rebalancing this portfolio. It came from a monthly rule on a
    three-region mix of index funds and was not a cost at all. On this portfolio&rsquo;s own yearly review the
    difference against never trading was <Figure id="rebalance-return" inline />, too small for the data to
    see, so the site books nothing for it either way.</p>

    <h3 id="two-portfolios">Numbers quoted for a slightly different portfolio</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-24</p>
    <p>The site described one portfolio and quoted numbers measured on a slightly different one, with five
    points more in the trend fund and five points less in the US index fund. Every page now quotes numbers for
    the portfolio it shows, and where a figure belongs to the other version it says so.</p>

    <h3 id="trend-premium">A trend-following return that had never been measured</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-22</p>
    <p>A forward return of <Figure id="trend-premium-withdrawn" inline showUnit={false} /> points a year for
    trend following sized the position and was then used to argue against holding it. It was a past average
    with a fee subtracted twice, on a different basis from the figure it was compared to. The number is gone and
    nothing replaced it: defensible estimates run from about 2.8 to 7.2 points a year, and picking one would be
    choosing the answer.</p>

    <h3 id="borrowed-comparison">A borrowed portfolio compared against an index that borrowed nothing</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-22</p>
    <p>The portfolio holds $1.32 of investments for every dollar put in and was compared against an index
    holding $1.00, which it beats in good years and trails in bad ones whatever else it does. Every comparison
    now also uses a control that borrows exactly as much.</p>

    <h3 id="cross-benchmark-sum">Savings added up across different comparisons</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-22</p>
    <p>Putting each fund in the account that taxes it least was said to be worth $38 to $55 a year on every
    $10,000. Two lines that exclude each other were both counted, and lines measured against different
    comparisons were summed. Against a sensible default it is worth
    <Figure id="placement-restated" inline showUnit={false} />, about $2 to $7 on every $10,000.</p>

    <h3 id="qualified-dividends">Every dividend assumed to be the good kind</h3>
    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>2026-08-22</p>
    <p>Fund dividends were assumed to qualify for the lower long-term tax rate. The funds&rsquo; own filings
    show most international dividends do not, which reversed which account the emerging-market funds belong in.
    Every account-placement result was worked again.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="pattern">What they have in common</h2>

    <p>Six of the eight were caught by the site arguing with itself; two by reading a cited source and finding
    it said something else. Most were correct when first calculated and broke when the number travelled to
    another page without its comparison, its window, or its range. That is why every number here carries its
    source.</p>

    <p>If you find one I missed, <a href="/about/">the about page</a> says how to report it.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
